champion:
That we too may live He lives again;
As our Champion behold Him stand,
Strong to save us, at God's Right Hand.

changing:
From their calm locks, and undiscomfited
Look stedfast truths against Time’s changing mask.
True, many a prophet teaches in the roads;

chart:
Four is enough, so let it be;
For the rest I make no chart,
There are no highroads to my heart;

changes:
She with swift progress cuts the azure plain,
Where grief subsides, where changes are no more,
And life's tumultuous billows cease to roar;

chalk:
Den dey all des lef hit to 'im, 'bout de trouble an' de talk;
An' howevah he decided, w'y dey bofe 'u'd walk de chalk;
But de fiah uz so 'sgusted how dey quoil an' dey shout

chafe:
Before my window sweep and sway,
And chafe in tortures of unrest.
My chin sinks down upon my breast;

chattered:
The kind hart's tears were falling;
Chattered the wistful stork;
Dove-voices with a dying fall

chatter:
Sappho's restriction was only the sky;
Ninon was ever the chatter of France;
But oh, what a good girl am I!

The snow may be a-flying o'er the meadow and the hill,
The ice has checked the chatter of the little laughing rill,
But in your cosey cradle you are warm and happy still;

chanting:
To eat the bishop’s wafer at the church;
From which long trail of chanting priests and girls,
A face flashed like a cymbal on his face,

charmed:
Were stately like the stars, and yet were tender;
Her figure charmed me like a windy stem
Quivering and drooped and slender.

chat:
To limber up my soul with song;
To stop awhile 'n' chat the men,
'N' drink some cider now an' then.

charities:
He seemed a fair young man, his eyes
Beaming with serious charities;
His cheek was white but hardly pale;

charred:
Through all the green hills to our fathers’ house,
You’d come upon a great charred circle where
The patient earth was singed an acre round;

challenge:
For me, the uprooted tree.
My challenge to the world is hurled;
The world must bow to me.

chastened:
Whose graves are thick whose ranks are thin.
Oh, people chastened in the fire,
To nobler, grander things aspire;

chanted:
While Night like a spirit steals up o'er the hills;
The thrush from his tree where he chanted all day,
No longer his music in ecstasy trills.

charms:
Through the gently yielding dough.
Maids may vaunt their finer charms--
Naught to me like Dinah's arms;

chafed:
One wrung foul ooze from his clustered hair;
Two chafed his hands, and did not spare;
But one held his drooping head breast-high,

chant:
There is a smell of spice and myrrh,
A bride-chant burdened with one name;
The bride-song rises steadier

challenging:
And hose and hydrant cannot here avail;
The flames laugh high and fling their challenging light,
And clouds turn gray and black from silver-pale.

changeful:
And not my sleeping sires,
Who gave the flame its changeful
And iridescent fires;

chattin:
Tappin' lak a hammah;
Jaybird chattin' wif a bee,
Tryin' to teach him grammah.

changin:
Folks is talkin' 'bout de money, 'bout de silvah an' de gold;
All de time de season 's changin' an' de days is gittin' cold.
An' dey 's wond'rin' 'bout de metals, whethah we'll have one er two.

chaih:
Wish 't she would n' mek me
Set down in dis chaih;
Pshaw, it would n't tek me

chattles:
then!
Beings once chattles now are men;
Over the gloom of slavery's night,

charg'd:
He long'd to see America excell;
He charg'd its youth that ev'ry grace divine
Should with full lustre in their conduct shine;

challeng'd:
"Decide the contest, and the victor's right."
Thus challeng'd he: all Israel stood amaz'd,
And ev'ry chief in consternation gaz'd;

chang'd:
Alphenor saw, and trembling at the view,
Beat his torn breast, that chang'd its snowy hue.
He flies to raise them in a kind embrace;

champa:
Mother mine, to the wild forest I am going,
Where upon the champa boughs the champa buds are blowing;
To the koil-haunted river-isles where lotus lilies glisten,

chanticleer:
I'd stand triumphant on one leg;
Like chanticleer I'd almost crow
To let our little neighbours know.

A spider at his trade again;
An added strut in chanticleer;
A flower expected everywhere;

changeth:
By day she woos me, soft, exceeding fair:
But all night as the moon so changeth she;
Loathsome and foul with hideous leprosy

chaffinch:
No pebble at your foot, but proves a sphere;
No chaffinch, but implies the cherubim:
And,—glancing on my own thin, veinéd wrist,—

chalcedony:
I saw his soul saw,—‘Jasper first,’ I said,
‘And second, sapphire; third, chalcedony;
The rest in order, ... last, an amethyst.’
